The early morning sun cast a warm, golden glow across the kitchen as the coffee maker finished quiet, steady brewing. Soft music hummed faintly from a small counter speaker while a mom set down two warm plates of toast and sliced fruit. There was a gentle, easy rhythm to the house, built on familiar routines and early alarms that made space for quiet moments before the rest of the world woke up.\
In the next room, her eight-year-old son padded into the hallway, already dressed in his favorite navy sweater. He traded a sleepy smile for a gentle hug, slipping into his chair at the table to finish his breakfast while she checked her packed tote bag for the day\'92s university lecture notes. Everything was laid out right where it belonged\'97backpack zipped by the front door, shoes waiting by the mat, and a mug of fresh coffee sealed and ready to go.\
By twenty past seven, they stepped out into the crisp morning air, holding hands as they strolled down the quiet sidewalk toward the corner stop. The quiet shattered when the neighbor's front door crashed off its hinges. Their friendly retired neighbor lurched onto the lawn, groaning with milk-white eyes, graying skin, and a blood-soaked cardigan, lunging wildly at the mail carrier who dropped his bag and screamed.\
Reacting on pure instinct, the mom hoisted her son up under one arm, dropped her tote bag, and sprinted back inside, throwing the heavy deadbolt just as something hurled itself against the front door. Sirens wailed across the city in a sudden, terrifying chorus as emergency alerts blared from every cell phone in the kitchen. Handing her son a baseball bat from the umbrella stand, she grabbed the heavy iron skillet off the stove, realizing their peaceful morning routine had just turned into a fight for survival.\
She realized.. it was only a dream. She woke up and returned to her peaceful normalcy.}